00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.676
So knowledge items are automatically extracted.

00:20:01.676 --> 00:20:06.596
Learnings that improve the accuracy and effectiveness
of future code transformations.

00:20:07.036 --> 00:20:12.836
So as a human reviewer, you can inspect these knowledge items,
determine which are broadly applicable,

00:20:12.836 --> 00:20:18.476
and enabling them for future runs,
thus making your agent measurably smarter over time.

00:20:19.356 --> 00:20:27.596
And lastly, you can also extend capabilities at every stage by
integrating with external systems through model context protocols.

00:20:28.676 --> 00:20:32.636
All right, so now let's see how AWS Transform custom works in action.

00:20:32.956 --> 00:20:35.996
I have a series of demo scenarios. And for the first one,

00:20:35.996 --> 00:20:43.276
I'll show you how you can upgrade a Java eight project
to Java 21 using AWS Managed transformation definitions.

00:20:43.436 --> 00:20:48.396
So I'll be using the command line interface
to list all of AWS managed Transformations.

00:20:48.436 --> 00:20:50.556
Review the Java upgrade transformation.

00:20:51.076 --> 00:20:58.156
Apply this project on a sample Java eight project, and then we'll
review the transformation plan as well as the validation process.

00:20:59.796 --> 00:21:03.036
All right so for this particular
demo I have my Visual Studio code open.

00:21:03.036 --> 00:21:06.196
And I'm going to upgrade a Java eight to Java 21 project.

00:21:06.316 --> 00:21:11.916
So our first run ADX custom dev list
to list all of the AWS managed definitions.

00:21:12.076 --> 00:21:16.596
And these definitions are stored in a registry
that's applicable only to your account.

00:21:16.836 --> 00:21:22.316
So if you take a look over here, Java version
upgrade is one of the transformation definitions available.

00:21:23.196 --> 00:21:29.196
Next, I run ADX Custom dev exec to execute
the Java version upgrade transformation.

00:21:29.316 --> 00:21:33.156
So if you take a look over here
we'll also specify a configuration file.

00:21:33.156 --> 00:21:40.996
Things like the repository path transformation name build
command validation command as well as additional plan context.

00:21:41.396 --> 00:21:46.516
Additional plan context is just a natural
language way of giving additional instructions.

00:21:46.596 --> 00:21:52.676
So for this particular example, I will say this is a Java
8 to 21 transformation and use a specific version

00:21:52.676 --> 00:21:57.756
when upgrading spring boot. So the next
thing I'll do is I'll execute the transformation.

00:21:57.956 --> 00:22:03.796
And what happens is that AWS Transform
custom will first review the project and create a plan.

00:22:04.196 --> 00:22:08.396
A plan is a definition of how the transformation
will be applied to your project.

00:22:08.396 --> 00:22:15.916
So in this example it's going to do Gradle wrapper upgrade and then
Spring Boot upgrade as well as deprecated API remediation.

00:22:16.396 --> 00:22:21.956
So you can then review and approve the plan
and then let the execution agent take over.

00:22:22.316 --> 00:22:26.436
So what is going to happen is that the execution
agent will start making the changes.

00:22:26.436 --> 00:22:32.196
And after it's completed, you will see a final
validation summary to confirm a successful build.

00:22:32.516 --> 00:22:37.836
It will also list down everything that has been done,
as well as the exit criteria that are being met.

00:22:39.756 --> 00:22:45.876
All right, so for the next demo, I'll show you how you can create
a custom transformation for your own specific scenario.

00:22:45.876 --> 00:22:49.636
So the previous demo was using out
of the box available transformations.

00:22:49.636 --> 00:22:52.956
But for now we'll see how you can create your custom transformation,

00:22:52.956 --> 00:22:55.716
publish your custom transformation to your registry

00:22:55.716 --> 00:23:00.636
so that other users can use that same definition
for their projects with similar use cases.

00:23:01.316 --> 00:23:05.156
We'll also apply this transformation on a Java 21 project.

00:23:05.356 --> 00:23:08.836
Review the transformation plan as well as the validation steps.

00:23:10.916 --> 00:23:14.316
All right, so for this demo the project is ready on Java 21.

00:23:14.316 --> 00:23:21.956
But now I'll modernize it to adopt newer performance improvements
for Java 21 and also update the Docker images to use newer ones.

00:23:22.916 --> 00:23:28.436
So I'll first open up AWS Transform
custom interactive mode and I'll say create a new transformation.

00:23:29.076 --> 00:23:32.236
Then I'll specify it to create a new transformation.

00:23:32.556 --> 00:23:38.276
And I'll say, I want you to modernize
this Java 21 project to use Java 21 features

00:23:38.276 --> 00:23:43.276
and also update container related files to use Java 21 images.

00:23:43.796 --> 00:23:49.316
So what happens is that AWS transform custom searches
for existing transformation definition,

00:23:49.516 --> 00:23:53.436
and you will find that none of it
specifically focused on my requirements.

00:23:53.436 --> 00:23:56.196
So then are prompted to create a new transformation.

00:23:56.676 --> 00:24:01.276
So you then ask if there's any specific
context or documentation I want to give it to.

00:24:01.276 --> 00:24:04.956
So I'll say use best practices for Java 21 modernization.

00:24:05.796 --> 00:24:12.676
So what happens is that a transformation definition
will be generated including clear objective entry criteria,

00:24:12.916 --> 00:24:16.156
step by step instructions as well as validation criteria.

00:24:16.676 --> 00:24:19.276
So essentially based on your requirements,

00:24:19.476 --> 00:24:26.516
AWS Transform custom actually creates for you your transformation
definition or the recipe that you can apply for multiple projects.

00:24:27.276 --> 00:24:34.356
Next, I'll also publish this transformation definition
to my account registry so that other users can also access this.

00:24:35.276 --> 00:24:39.356
And after publishing it, I will apply
this transformation onto my project.

00:24:39.836 --> 00:24:43.276
And the transformation agent will generate a step by step plan.

00:24:43.516 --> 00:24:50.516
So if you take a look over here, the plan includes things like
records, conversion, textbox modernization, Docker modernization,

00:24:50.516 --> 00:24:54.356
so on and so forth. It will also tell you what is not applicable.

00:24:54.356 --> 00:24:58.476
So for this particular project,
for example, virtual threads is not applicable.

00:24:58.476 --> 00:25:00.000
So you can review this. So I'll say looks good.

